    "body": "<!-- claude-projects-attribution: {\"github_login\":\"siainbuletin\"} -->\n_Requested by **Sia** · [project thread](https://claude.ai/code/project/chan_01KaQfLZaF43QUMN3kcT7pW2?thread=cmsg_01KaQfLZaF43QUMN3kcT7pW28jXjUaoq76mQCkZLcJxtaz)_\n\n#174 set `vertico-posframe-font` to enlarge the text. Sia reports it draws the scratch buffer and the dashboard in the same window, overlapping, and `M-x` cannot open the posframe until `(vertico-posframe-mode -1)` is evaluated.\n\n## Changes\n- Removes `sp-posframe-update-font`, the `vertico-posframe-font` setting and its `fontaine-set-preset-hook` line from `sp-posframe`.\n- Removes the now unused `sp-minibuffer-face` and its fontaine keys from `sp-fonts`. No text scale is left in the minibuffer.\n- `sp-posframe` doc: new \"Text size\" section with the failure, the research on whether `fontaine` is the cause, and what to try next; tables and rebuild plan updated.\n- CHANGELOG entry linking the doc section.\n\n## Is it fontaine?\nProbably not (read from the sources of fontaine, posframe and vertico-posframe; not run). `fontaine` only sets faces and knows nothing about child frames. `posframe` sizes and positions the child frame with the parent's `default-font-width` and `default-line-height`, so a larger font in the child needs more room than it gets. The doc lists what is not ruled out (font-spec vs string, points vs pixels) and three next steps.\n\nNot run in Emacs (none in the container).\n\n---\n_Generated by [Claude Code](https://claude.ai/code/session_01WWLNi8ooNzim25iTWhixoA)_",
